## Introduction
Heterogeneous Brønsted acid catalysts are tremendously important in industrial processes that have been widely used to make important chemicals and fuels.[1,2] However, it remains a big synthetic challenge to prepare strong solid Brønsted acid catalysts for challenging reactions, particularly for enantioselective transformations.[3,4] Chiral imidodiphosphoric acid (IDP) catalysts, pioneered by List,[5] exhibit strong Brønsted acidity, and have recently emerged as powerful Brønsted acid catalysts for many enantioselective processes, especially for the small and unfunctionalized substrates.[6-11] However, the rotational freedom of the P-N bond results in a dynamic transformation between O,O-syn and O,O-anti conformers and low enantioselectivity because of the geometrically different acid/base pairs (Figure 1a).[5] So, the sterically highly-demanding groups are always required to rigidify the IDP to adopt a single O,O-syn conformation, which is favorable for enantiocontrol (Figure 1b). In this work, by an alternative way, we demonstrated that conformations of IDPs in metal-organic frameworks (MOFs)
can be modulated via topology tuning to boost enantioselectivity in heterogeneous asymmetric catalysis (Figure 1c).
MOFs have shown great application potential in diverse areas including heterogeneous catalysis owing to their tunable porous structures.[12-23] Reticular chemistry has been extensively applied for the design and synthesis of MOFs with different topologies,[24,25] which can be exploited to alter the position and orientation of organic building blocks and regulate their physicochemical properties.[26-28] However, it is rare to find examples where the catalytic activity/selectivity stems from a spatial distribution of potential active sites that is achievable only within a specific framework.[29,30] By sterically protecting Brønsted acids from coordinating with metal ions, we and others have demonstrated that the chiral phosphoric acids can be incorporated into MOFs to enable a variety of asymmetric transformations including transfer hydrogenation reaction, Friedel-Crafts reaction, and iso-Pictet-Spengler reaction.[31-34] However, the open microenvironments around the active acid sites can hardly stabilize the transition states of the reactions involving small substrates. Moreover, the moderate Brønsted acidity severely limits their applications in the transformation of weak basic functional groups.[4,23] Here we addressed these issues by engineering chiral IDPs in highly stable and porous DyMOF with different topological structures that exhibited higher Brønsted acidity than the free IDPs.
Functionalization of axially chiral 1,1'-biphenol enabled the synthesis of a tetracarboxylate linker $(H_{4}L)$ to make highly stable and porous Dy-MOFs with different topologies. Although the ligands adopt a 4-connected (4-c) or 3-c coordination mode in the MOFs, all IDP cores take a single $^{O,O}$ -syn conformation. The space-restricted chiral microenvironments around the IDP active sites involving ligand conformations, cavity sizes, and hydrophobic effect can be modulated through the choice of network topology. This enables the Brønsted acid catalysis of the challenging and less developed asymmetric $^{O,O}$ acetalization of diol and aldehyde, as well as the asymmetric N,N-acetalization of 2-aminobenzamide and aldehyde with high enantioselectivity.
## Results and Discussion
The reaction of $S)$ -6,6'-dimethyl-3,3'-di-tert-butyl-5,5'- bis(methoxycarbonylphenyl) -1,1'-biphenyl-2,2'-diol and ${\mathsf{P O C l}}_{3}$ in ${\mathsf{C H}}_{2}{\mathsf{C l}}_{2}$ with triethylamine as the base afforded the desired phosphoryl chloride, followed by treatment with liquid ammonia to give the phosphorylamide. Condensation of the phosphoryl chloride and phosphorylamide in the presence of NaH as the base generated $(S,S)\ –M e_{4}L$ , followed by saponification to afford the target chiral IDP-derived tetracarboxylate linker $(S,S)\mathrel{-}\mathsf{H}_{4}\mathsf{L}$ with a unique double axially-chirality in a moderate yield. This type of double axially-chiral ligands has been rarely explored to make MOFs, which will greatly enrich the library of chiral MOFs.[35] On the other hand, lanthanide ions are attractive building blocks for making MOFs with different topologies because they can form diverse adaptable polynuclear clusters.[36,37] As such, the solvothermal reaction of $\mathsf{D y}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ and $(S,S)\mathrel{-}\mathsf{H}_{4}\mathsf{L}$ in $N,N_{\mathrm{\ell}}^{\prime}$ dimethylformamide (DMF) containing acetic acid as the modulator afforded rod-shaped single crystals of  1-Dy.
1-Dy crystallizes in the chiral tetragonal space group $14_{1}22$ with unit cell of $a=b=39.9001$ (9) Å, $c=31.4985(6)~{\AA}$ , and $V=$ 50146(2) $\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{3}$ . There are three crystallographically independent $\mathsf{D}\mathsf{y}^{3+}$ ions and two half-ligands in the asymmetric unit (Figure S1). The Dy1 ion adopts a distorted octahedral coordination geometry to bind four oxygen atoms of ${\mathsf{O}}{=}{\mathsf{P}}$ from two separated ligands and two oxygen atoms of two water molecules at the axial position, thus generating a 8-c metalloligand $\mathsf{D}\mathsf{y}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{2}\mathsf{L}_{2}$ (Figure S2 and Figure 2a). The eight-coordinated ${\mathsf{D}}\mathsf{y}2$ ion adopts a distorted trigonal dodecahedral geometry, which bonds to eight oxygen atoms from two chelating and four bridging carboxylate groups (Figure S2). Two adjacent Dy2 ions are connected by four carboxylate groups from four $L^{4-}$ and four bridging carboxylate groups from two metalloligands, forming a 6-c dinuclear $\mathsf{D y}_{2}(\mathsf{C O O})_{8}$ secondary building unit (SBU) (Figure 2a). Meanwhile, the Dy3 ion is coordinated to six oxygen atoms from four carboxylate groups and one oxygen atom from one water molecule (Figure S2). Two neighboring Dy3 ions are bridged by the two carboxylate groups and one $\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ to form an infinite 1D triple-strand helical rod-shaped $\mathsf{D}\mathsf{y}(\mathsf{O H})(\mathsf{C O}_{2}^{-})_{2}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})$ SBU with a translation distance of $31.50\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ (Figure 2a). As a result, the chiral IDP linkers $L^{4-}$ and the metalloligands are cross-linked by the dinuclear clusters and infinite 1D helical chains to create an intricate chiral three-periodic framework (Figure 2b). This is a rare example that the organic linkers and in situ formed metalloligands were incorporated into multicomponent chiral MOFs with high complexity in a single phase.[38,39]
One of the notable features of 1-Dy is that a unique triple helix is located in the channels of the framework. Each chiral IDP linker bonds to two dinuclear SBUs via carboxylate-M coordination to generate a $\scriptstyle4_{1}$ right-handed helical strand with a pitch of $94.5\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ (Figure 2d). Three identical helical chains, running around the $4_{1}$ screw axis, are intertwined with each other to form a right-handed triple helix along the $\boldsymbol{c}$ axis. The catalytic-active IDP groups are arranged periodically within the helical channels with a size of about $7.8\times7.8\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{2}$ , which were generated from intertwined triplehelix (Figure S3). The conformation of the triple-helix was critically depended on the double-axial chirality of the ligands. Moreover, the abundant tert-butyl groups decorated inside the surface of the helical channel endow the hydrophobic feature of the channel. This structure is highly similar to the structures of collagen, which is a triple-helix protein made of repeating prolinerich tripeptides.[40] It is worth noting that the mimicking of the collagen-type triple-helix is rather rare.[41,42] Topologically, the 4-c linker, 8-c metalloligand, together with the 8-c dinuclear cluster and 1D infinite rod SBU to form a (4,6,6,8)-c network with an unprecedented topology $\{4^{2.}5^{4}\}8\{5^{10.}7{\cdot}8^{4}\}\{5^{4.}8^{6.}9^{3.}11^{2}\}\{5^{5.}7\}^{2}$ (Figure 2c,e). Calculation by PLATON software reveals that $54.9\%$ of the total volume of 1-Dy can be accessed by guest molecules.[43]
It is well-known that the modulator has a big influence on the structure and crystallization of MOFs.[44] Especially, the use of 2- fluorobenzoic acid (2-FBA) as the modulator can prevent the formation of the commonly observed chainlike rare earth building units and facilitate the in situ formation of highly connected polynuclear carboxylate-based clusters.[45] As anticipated, with the modulation of 2-FBA, the solvothermal reactions between (S,S)-H4L and $\mathsf{D y}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ for $24\ h$ yielded 2-Dy as large colorless polyhedral crystals. Due to the extremely weak diffraction for 2-Dy, the crystal structures cannot be precisely determined through single-crystal X-ray diffraction (SCXRD). Fortunately, a successful SCXRD analysis was performed for the scandium analogue. Powder X-ray diffraction study reveals that 2-Dy and 2-Sc share the same structure (Figure 4b). So, the crystal structure of the Sc-analogue is described in detail below.
2-Sc also crystallizes in chiral space group $14_{1}22$ but with a totally different structure from 1-Dy. The $\mathsf{S c}^{3+}$ ion adopts an octahedral coordination geometry, which bonds to four oxygen atoms from neighboring carboxylate groups, one $O^{2\cdot}$ , and one water (Figure S2). Four $\mathsf{S c}^{3+}$ ions are held together by eight carboxylate groups to form a novel SBU $\mathsf{S c}_{4}(\mathsf{\Pi}\upmu_{2}\cdot\mathsf{O})_{2}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{4}(\mathsf{C O}_{2})_{8}$ with $C_{2}$ symmetry (Figure 3a). Although rectangular $[\mathsf{L}\mathsf{n}_{4}(\mathsf{\upmu}_{3}{\cdot}\mathsf{O})_{2}]^{8-}$ and $T_{\mathrm{d}}$ symmetric $[\mathsf{L}\mathsf{n}_{4}(\mathsf{\upmu}_{3}\mathsf{-O H})_{4}]^{8-}$ are reported as structural motifs in many Ln-MOFs,[49,50] to the best of our knowledge, this nonplanar $\mathsf{S c}_{4}$ SBU is firstly observed in Ln-MOFs. This inorganic SBU connects eight carboxylates from six separated linkers and can be viewed as a planar $6-c$ node. The chiral IDP linker adopts a T-shaped conformation, in which two parallel carboxylate groups coordinate to one tetranuclear cluster and the remained two carboxylates in a straight line bind to other two separated SBUs, which can be simplified as a 3-c node (Figure 3a). Thus, the 3-c chiral linkers are linked by planar 6-c SBUs to form a 3D (3,6)-c network (Figure 3b). Topological analysis of the obtained crystal structure revealed an unprecedented $\{4^{2}{\cdot}6\}^{2}\{4^{4}{\cdot}6^{2}{\cdot}8^{2}{\cdot}10^{7}\}$ topology (Figure 3c). According to the reticular chemistry, the combination of planar 6-c and 3-c nodes exclusively generated cys topology. Thus, 2-Sc represents the extremely rare MOFs built from planar $6-c$ and 3-c building blocks.
Taking a closer look at the crystal structure, we can find that 18 ligands and $10\ \mathsf{S c}_{4}$ clusters are held together to form a large diamond cage with a size of approximately $43.9\times29.5\times23.3\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{3}$ (Figure S4). The diamond cages are interconnected with each other to form a 3D framework with 1D square channels with a size of $18.0~\times~18.0~\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{2}$ (Figure 3d,e). The corresponding solventaccessible free volume for 2-Sc was estimated to be $76.3\%$ by using PLATON software.[43]
A comparison of the crystal structures of 1 and 2 shows the chiral IDP linker adopts different coordination modes. For example, the chiral IDP linker acts as a 4-c node in 1, but functions as a 3-c T node in 2. Moreover, the IDP linkers display different arrangements in the crystalline structures. In 1, the chiral linkers are interconnected to form entwined triple-helix structures. The catalytically-active sites are aligned and oriented toward the confined hydrophobic channels. Although the IDP linkers are arranged with a helix conformation in 2, the active IDP sites are arranged outside and towards the large pores that are accessible to guest molecules. Therefore, by simply altering the modulating agents, we have realized the fine-tuning of the microenvironment for the active sites, thus providing an excellent platform to explore the relationship between the chiral microenvironments and catalytic performances.
The framework topology indeed influences the overall conformation of the organocatalyst ligand in the crystals. The aromatic rings of the biphenyl units have almost identical dihedral angles $(\phi)$ of $65.5^{\circ}$ and $65.7^{\circ}$ for 1-Dy and 2-Sc, respectively, but, in the IDP core, the torsion angles (θ) between the two adjacent free O atoms around the linkage joining the P atoms are quite different $38.3^{\circ}$ vs $22.9^{\circ}\ .$ ) (Table 1). Thus, the ligand L in 1-Dy is less compact and more variable in shape than that in 2-Sc, as further indicated by the distances of the adjacent free $0^{\ldots}0$ atoms $(d_{1})$ and $\mathtt{O^{...}C}$ (tert-butyl) $(d_{2})$ in the IDP cores, both of which are obviously longer for 1-Dy then those of 2-Sc (Table 1). This is consistent with that the ligands adopt 4-c and 3-c T-shaped coordination modes in the two structures, respectively. So, the conformations and local microenvironments of IDP units can be manipulated by tuning the MOF topologies.
1-Dy and 2-Dy were stable in air and insoluble in water and common organic solvents. The phase purity of bulk samples was established by comparison of their observed and simulated powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns (Figure 4a,b). Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images for 1-Dy and 2-Dy showed a homogeneous morphology of rod-shaped and polyhedral-shaped crystals, respectively, further indicating the phase purity of the bulk samples (Figure S16). Circular dichroism (CD) spectra of them made from (R,R) and $(S,S)\bot$ are mirror images of each other, indicating the enantiomeric nature of the frameworks (Figure S5). Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) revealed that the guest molecules could be readily removed in the temperature range of 80 to $150^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and the decomposition of the framework starts at $450^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ (Figure S6). The permanent porosity was examined by nitrogen adsorption measurements at $77\mathsf{K}$ The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface areas were calculated to be 1316 and $1864\mathsf{m}^{2}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ for 1-Dy and 2-Dy, respectively (Figures 4c and S8).
Given the great significance of the Brønsted acidity of solid catalysts for catalysis, the Hammett indicator method was used to measure the Brønsted acidity of these two chiral MOFs as well as the related molecular catalyst.[51] As shown in Figure 5, after the incorporation of chiral IDP into MOFs, the significantly enhanced Brønsted acidity was observed $-3.2<\mathsf{H}_{0}<-3.0$ and $-3.0<mathsf{H}_{0}<-$ 2.4 for 1-Dy and 2-Dy, respectively). Remarkably, 1-Dy showed a color change in a benzene solution of dicinnamlalcetone, indicating a higher Brønsted acidity than the reported chiral phosphoric acid MOFs (Table S3).[34,35] The coordination-driven self-assembly of chiral IDP catalysts into MOFs is capable of increasing their Brønsted acidity, thus leading to the generation of porous solid catalysts for asymmetric catalysis.
Chiral acetals have widely existed in diverse natural products and pharmaceuticals.[49] The presence of chiral strong Brønsted acidic sites within the confined environments for these two MOFs motivated us to investigate their catalytic performances in asymmetric acetalization reactions including N,N-actetalization and $^{O,O}$ -acetalization reactions.
Initially, we evaluated the catalytic activity of the MOFs in catalyzing the asymmetric N,N-acetalization reaction of 2- aminobenzamide and aldehyde to produce optically-pure 2,3- dihydroquinazolinone (DHQZ), which is a privileged scaffold in various natural products and medicinally active compounds due to their important pharmacological activities.[50] After screening various reaction conditions including solvent, temperature, and reaction time, we observed that 1-Dy can smoothly promote the condensation and cyclization of 2-aminobenzamide with 3- hydroxybenzaldehyde. Especially, $5\ m o l\%$ loading of 1-Dy can catalyze the condensation/amine addition of 2-aminobenzamide and 3-hydroxybenzaldehyde to generate the desired products with $89\%$ yield and $99\%$ ee in toluene at $40~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ after $24\ h$ (Table S4, entries 1-7). No background reaction was observed without the addition of the 1-Dy, thus proving the significant role of 1-Dy for this reaction (Table S4, entry 8). Moreover, by using dilute triethylamine solution treated 1-Dy as the catalyst, no conversion was observed, underlining that the Brønsted acid protons inside the framework are vital for this transformation (Table S4, entry 9). Having the optimized conditions in hands, we extended the scope of 2-aminobenzamide. As shown in Table 2, the substituents in the 2-aminobenzamide have little influence on the yields and enantioselectivities of the products. The corresponding chiral N,N-acetals were generated in $83-91\%$ yields and $92-99\%$ ee (Table 2, entries 1, 4, 7 and 10-12). Then, we tried to explore the aldehyde scope. The protecting of the hydroxyl group with methyl gave extremely decreased ee values of the desired product (Table 2, entry 14). When the benzaldehyde was used as the substrate, the corresponding 2,3-dihydroquinazolinone was obtained in $85\%$ yield and $50\%$ ee under identical conditions (Table 2, entry 13). We also performed the reaction by using 2- and 4-hydroxyl benzaldehyde. The corresponding DHQZ were obtained in 78 and $74\%$ yield with 40 and $48\%$ ee, respectively (Table 2, entries 15 and 16). These results indicated that the position of the hydroxyl group at benzaldehyde has a significant effect on the enantioselectivity of the products. The hydroxyl group at the 3-position of benzaldehyde can form a hydrogenbonding interaction with the IDP groups to stabilize the intermediate, thus leading to enhanced enantiocontrol (Figure S14).[51]
Under identical conditions, 2-Dy can also catalyze this reaction, affording the desired products in $88-92\%$ yields with $78\mathrm{-}82\%$ ee (Table 2, entries 2, 5, and 8). The ee values are typically lower than those obtained by 1-Dy. Kinetic studies further confirmed that 2-Dy exhibited higher activity but lower enantiocontrol than 1- Dy (Figure S11). From the single-crystal structures, we can observe that the catalytically active IDP groups of 2-Dy point toward the large pores whereas the Brønsted acids in 1-Dy are placed in a confined environment. Thus, 2-Dy is favorable for substrate and product diffusion but is unfavorable for enantiodiscrimination, thus resulting in decreased ee values.
To investigate the contribution of the framework structures to asymmetric catalysis, we evaluated the catalytic performances of the homogeneous analogue $M e_{4}L$ . Control experiments showed that $5\mathrm{\mol}\%$ loading of $M e_{4}L$ can promote the asymmetric transformation to afford the target products in a little higher yields but significantly decreased ee values than 1-Dy (Table 2, entries 3, 6, and 9). To further understand the reaction detail, kinetic experiments were performed using 2-aminobenzamide and 3- hydroxylbenzaldehyde. It was observed that the reaction catalyzed by $M e_{4}L$ was faster than that catalyzed by 1-Dy, but with much lower enantioselectivity in the whole process (Figure S11). The rotational freedom of the P-N bond renders relative flexible positioning of catalytic sites for the catalyst molecule to generate diverse conformations, thus giving lower enantiocontrol. After assembling into MOFs, the frameworks rigidified the molecules to form only one distinct and geometrically fixed acid/base pair, which is a prerequisite for excellent enantioselectivity. Moreover, the catalytic sites together with the Dy ions and tert-butyl groups in 1-Dy created highly congested chiral microenvironments in the MOFs and are also responsible for the high enantioselectivity.
To investigate the role of MOF channels in catalysis, the bulky substrate 3,5-dibenzyloxybenzaldehyde was subjected to the reaction. Only $<5\%$ yield of the product was detected catalyzed by 1-Dy (Table 3, entry 17), which was much lower than the $92\%$ yield obtained with Me4L (Table 3, entry 18). The extremely low yield is probably attributed to the extremely bulky substrate that cannot access the catalytically active Brønsted acid sites in the MOF cavity through the windows because of its large diameter (Figure S13). Taken together, the above results indicated that the reaction mainly occurs inside the pores.
Multiple experiments were performed to confirm the heterogeneous and recyclable nature of the 1-Dy catalyst. First, after the completion of the reaction of 2-aminobenzamide and 3- hydroxybenzaldehyde, 1-Dy can be recovered by simple filtration and reused at least 8 repeated times without significant loss of its catalytic activity and enantioselectivity (Figure S9). Second, 1-Dy recovered from the reaction remained crystalline and structrally intact, as indicated by the PXRD pattern (Figure 4a). Besides, after 8 catalytic recycles, the BET surface area of $1195\ \mathsf{m}^{2}/\mathsf{g}$ is only slightly lower than that of the pristine 1-Dy (Figure 4c). Third, only about $0.06\%$ of the Dy species were detected in the supernatant by ICP-MS.
Compared to the well-established enantioselective generation of chiral N,N-acetals, the asymmetric additions to oxocarbenium ions intermediates to obtain $^{O,O}$ -acetals are more challenging and much less developed, probably owing to the low reactivity and inferior enantioselective control.[7,49] We assumed that 1-Dy with strong Brønsted acidity and a more confined environment can efficiently catalyze the asymmetric acetalization reaction to generate the $^{O,O}$ acetals. After optimizing the various reaction conditions including solvent, temperature, and reaction time, we found that $5\mathrm{mol}\%$ 1-Dy can promote the catalytic asymmetric acetalization reaction between 2-methylpropane-1,2-diol and benzaldehyde to generate the target $^{O,O}$ acetals in $79\%$ yield and $90\%$ ee at $80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ after $48\mathrm{~h~}$ (Table S5, entries 1-9). Control experiments confirmed the vital role of Brønsted acid prontons in 1-Dy for this reaction (Table S5, entries 10 and 11). Having established satisfactory reaction conditions in hands, we tried to expand the scope of this methodology by using various aldehydes The substituents on the aromatic ring of the aldehydes do not significantly affect the catalytic performance, and the chiral $0,O-$ acetals were obtained in $73\%$ yields and $89-95\%$ ee (Table 3, entries 1, 4, 7, 10, and 11). The 3-methylbutane-1,3-diol was used as the substrate to generate the corresponding sixmembered acetal in $79\%$ yield and $96\%$ ee values (Table 3, entry 10).
Under identical conditions, 2-Dy can also promote this reaction and afford the target products $80-82\%$ yields with $71-76\%$ ee (Table 3, entries 2, 5, and 8). Again, homogeneous control $M e_{4}L$ displayed significantly lower enantioselectivities than 1-Dy (Table 3, entries 3, 6, and 9), further illustrating the superiority of the MOF catalyst. To ascertain that the substrates access the internal IDP sites via the open channels, the bulky 3,5- dibenzyloxybenzaldehyde was subjected to the reaction (Figure S13). As anticipated, the yield of the product catalyzed by 1-Dy was $<5\%$ (Table 3, entry 13), which is much lower than that the $81\%$ yield produced by $M e_{4}L$ (Table 3, entry 14), indicating that the reaction was associated with the substrate being bound in the pores. Several tests were performed to prove the heterogeneous nature of 1-Dy in the above asymmetric acetalization reaction. 1- Dy can be readily recycled and reused for at least 8 runs with negligible loss of efficiency and enantioselectivity (Figures 4d and S10), and the recovered catalyst retained crystallinity and remained structurally intact, as revealed by PXRD and BET surface area measurements (Figure 4a, 4c).
Various chiral solid Brønsted acids including solid phosphoric acid have been employed as heterogeneous catalysts for the asymmetric synthesis of DHQZ, however, in most cases, only moderate to excellent enantioselectivities can be realized.[31,32,52,53] The ee values of the present IDP MOF-based protocol are higher or comparable to those of reported chiral phosphoric acid-based heterogeneous catalysts and even homogeneous chiral catalysts, as summarized in Table S6.[54-58] Furthermore, none of these heterogeneous solid acid catalysts were used for promoting the asymmetric O,O-acetalization reaction, and our results were comparable to the homogeneous system in terms of both the activity and enantioselectivity (Table S7).[7]
## Conclusion
In summary, we have prepared two highly strong Brønsted-acidic Ln-MOFs with high chemical stability and two different topologies using an enantiopure 1,1'-biphenol IDP-based tetracarboxylate linkers, whose conformations and local chiral microenvironments are controlled by the framework topology. In both structures, the IDP moieties are uniformly distributed within the helical channels with different sizes and shapes and display greatly enhanced Brønsted acidity compared to the free IDP. Despite the fixed ligands can adopt a 4-c or 3-c T binding mode, all IDP cores adopt only a single type of catalytically relevant $^{O,O}$ syn conformation. The MOF containing 4-c IDP that takes a relative less compact shape can be highly efficient and enantioselective Brønsted acid catalysts to promote the challenging asymmetric O,Oacetalization of diols and aldehydes, as well as the asymmetric N,N-acetalization of 2-aminobenzamides and aldehydes. The MOF catalyst could be recycled and reused without a noticeable decrease in both activity and selectivity. This work thus highlights the great potential of MOFs to improve the enantioselectivity of molecular catalysts with a dynamic structure by modulating their conformations and chiral microenvironments and facilitates the rational design of strong solid Brønsted acids for challenging asymmetric transformations.
